# Line endings — declared once, so "the file changed" means the CONTENT changed.
#
# Why this file exists (issue #82):
#
#   The repo was developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`. The index and
#   CI store LF, but that machine's working tree holds CRLF, so any gate that
#   compares raw bytes reports drift forever. `npm run skin:check` compares the
#   shipped `SKINS` block against a fresh build of the design system — a real
#   gate (it is the only thing watching byte/format hygiene, the value drift is
#   watched by `tests/skin.quality.test.cjs`), and it was red for reasons that
#   had nothing to do with the design system. A gate that is red for
#   content-free reasons is a gate people learn to ignore.
#
#   Declaring `eol=lf` here makes every checkout — Windows, macOS, Linux, CI —
#   produce the same bytes, so `npm run skin:check` and the preview fingerprint
#   gate (#83) can compare without a tolerance.
#
# Do not remove the catch-all: a new text extension added without a rule here
# is exactly how the CRLF working tree creeps back in. `text=auto` still lets
# git detect and skip real binaries.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.js     text eol=lf
*.cjs    text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.ts     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.patch  text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf

# Assets: never "converted", never diffed. A line-ending pass that rewrites a
# PNG is data loss, not hygiene.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
